  "market_context": "GBP/USD 4-hour chart during May–August period, showing a ranging market with repeated rejections at a top resistance level and a lower support zone; analyst addresses common trader confusion about S/R drawing techniques.",
  "key_observation": "The analyst observes that drawing S/R as compact zones (containing most wicks and closes) is preferred over single lines or multiple messy lines, and warns against overly large zones that cause confusion.",
  "analysis_steps": [
    "Step 1: The analyst introduces the question of whether to draw S/R as lines or zones, using the GBP/USD 4H chart’s top resistance level as the example.",
    "Step 2: The analyst demonstrates the first method: a single solid horizontal line through the resistance area, noting it works only if it touches all reversal points.",
    "Step 3: The analyst shows the second method: multiple horizontal lines drawn from individual candle closes, highlighting that this makes the chart messy and confusing.",
    "Step 4: The analyst presents the preferred method: drawing a compact horizontal zone that contains most candle bodies, wicks, and rejection points—emphasizing it should not be oversized.",
    "Step 5: The analyst applies the same zone-drawing logic to a lower support level, explicitly excluding distant candles to avoid an excessively wide zone, and identifies a nearby fake breakout."
  ],
  "outcome": null,
  "lessons": "Draw S/R as compact, clean zones that encompass most price reactions (closes and wicks); avoid single precise lines (too rigid) and multiple lines or oversized zones (too messy/confusing). Price action confirmation matters more than exact line placement.",
